186530747 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530747 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530747 1072 #uuid "2c0d33b2-47e5-55ae-b760-568401b27bca" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530747 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,3\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,3)-f(0,1) \\\\  & =\\left(- \\left(1\\right) \\left(3\\right)\\right)-\\left(- \\left(0\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530748 1080 "3" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530748 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530748 1083 #uuid "32f421d3-0ff0-50e3-990e-8e56151c2e33" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530749 1080 "1 - e^{\\pi^{2}}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530749 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530749 1083 #uuid "373cde8b-2ba1-502e-9e79-9a586dd3b83b" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530750 1080 "4 - 4 e^{\\pi^{2}}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530750 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530750 1083 #uuid "3cfec755-6997-5f15-9fed-98e9c1c3f129" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530751 1080 "9" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530751 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530751 1083 #uuid "4852ea1f-8754-5a01-89c0-e3f5916b4530" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530752 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530764 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530752 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530752 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t + 1,t + 1\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(x\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530752 1072 #uuid "4d5a26a2-4d59-5e5b-a1a3-1666d9c373b2" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530752 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =x, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{x^{2}}{2} + y^{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 2,2\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(2,2)-f(1,1) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{\\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(2\\right)^{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2}+\\left(1\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\  & =2+4- \\frac{1}{2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530753 1080 "0" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530753 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530753 1083 #uuid "542a3357-432b-562c-913e-663a74e70ad4" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530754 1080 "-6" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530754 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530754 1083 #uuid "56eb8950-e15e-52f0-ae16-fb7586994618" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530755 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530757 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530755 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530755 1071 "Consider the curve $C$, defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nEvaluate $\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r$ for $F(x,y)=\\left(- 3 y\\right)\\,i+\\left(- 3 x\\right)\\,j.$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to use the fact that $F$ is conservative.*" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530755 1072 #uuid "5742e24a-185d-570b-8c93-c58d8a73320b" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530755 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ such that $F=\\nabla f.$ Since $F$ is conservative, we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =- 3 y, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =- 3 x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=- 3 x y.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $\\nabla f=F.$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(1) & =\\langle 1,1\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(1,1)-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(- 3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(1\\right)\\right)-\\left(- 3 \\left(0\\right) \\left(0\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =-3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530756 1080 "-18" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530756 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530756 1083 #uuid "5a844f1c-aba0-5582-9d00-7703dff22261" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530748 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530754 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530760 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530770 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530774 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1077 "selection" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530774 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530757 1079 #uuid "639fe49f-92ca-5726-bea7-608bd05da2a6" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530758 1080 "- \\frac{9}{2}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530758 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530758 1083 #uuid "64249498-a798-535b-9188-cb51e727cda9" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530759 1080 "\\frac{11}{4}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530759 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530759 1083 #uuid "7985ce09-6e30-561e-a33e-cc009dbcd028" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530760 1080 "- \\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530760 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530760 1083 #uuid "8e134253-f04d-5625-a0ee-1441bd7c69d6" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530761 1080 "\\frac{11}{2}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530761 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530761 1083 #uuid "97a9befc-e3ed-5fa8-9702-f38ae767d815" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530762 1080 "- \\frac{3}{2}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530762 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530762 1083 #uuid "998816b6-609b-5a13-94bc-6d1fc0091e92" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530763 1080 "-2 + 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530763 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530763 1083 #uuid "a8b7102d-f815-5dd3-8623-99aa71c67037" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530742 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530744 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530758 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530766 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1075 #edb/ref 17592186530779 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1077 "selection" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1078 #edb/ref 17592186530779 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530764 1079 #uuid "b5939e5b-1a1e-5cfa-9cdb-592cf05e8314" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530765 1068 #edb/ref 17592186530738 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530765 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530765 1071 "Consider the vector field $F,$ defined as\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)\\,i+\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)\\,j.\n$$\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the curve $y=x^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(x \\right)}$ for $x\\in [0,\\pi].$" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530765 1072 #uuid "bc21e937-2785-5c98-a85b-95973fde2ff7" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530765 1074 "Let $C$ be a piecewise-smooth curve given by the vector function $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b].$ Let $f$ be a differentiable function of two or three variables such that $∇f$ is continuous on $C.$ The fundamental theorem for line integrals states that \n\n$$\n\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r=f(r(b))-f(r(a)).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we define $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)$ as follows:\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=\\underbrace{\\left(y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)}_{P(x,y)}\\,i+\\underbrace{\\left(2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}\\right)}_{Q(x,y)}\\,j.\n$$\n\nTo apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we must first find a potential function $f$ (if it exists) such that $F=∇f.$ Notice that the domain of $F$ is ${R}^{2},$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=2 y \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ there exists a potential function $f$ such that $F=∇f,$ and we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f_x(x,y) & =y^{2} \\cos{\\left(x \\right)}, \\\\ f_y(x,y) & =2 y \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nReconstructing the function $f$ from its gradient in the usual way, we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=y^{2} \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 2 e^{y}.\n$$\n\nNote that we set $C=0$ for the arbitrary constant since we can use any function $f$ that satisfies $∇f=F.$\n\nWe're now ready to apply the fundamental theorem for line integrals. A suitable parametrization of the curve $C$ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=\\langle t,t^{2} + 2 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\rangle,\\,t\\in [0,\\pi].\n$$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of the curve are given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =\\langle 0,0\\rangle, \\\\ r(\\pi) & =\\langle \\pi,\\pi^{2}\\rangle.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the endpoints of the path defined by $C$ are $(0,0)$ and $(\\pi,\\pi^{2}).$\n\nFinally, applying the fundamental theorem for line integrals, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_C F\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r & =\\int_C\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathrm{d}r \\\\  & =f(\\pi,\\pi^{2})-f(0,0) \\\\  & =\\left(\\left(\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(\\pi\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(\\pi^{2}\\right)}\\right)-\\left(\\left(0\\right)^{2} \\sin{\\left(\\left(0\\right) \\right)}- 2 e^{\\left(0\\right)}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0- 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}\\right)-\\left(0-2\\right) \\\\  & =2 - 2 e^{\\pi^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530766 1080 "\\frac{9}{4}" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530766 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530766 1083 #uuid "bfbf1197-1026-5682-934d-702aeaecd9ee" 13194139535827 17592186045443 true] [17592186530767 1068 #e